// Awaitable — the consumption side of the async model (ASYNC.md,
// "Awaitable trait"). Park-flavored by design: `wait` MAY park the current
// context (inside a fiber it parks the fiber and frees its worker; on a raw
// thread it blocks). A Rust-style poll/Waker trait is deliberately rejected —
// it would smuggle the state-machine transform back in through the trait
// definition.
//
// `Task<T>` is the conformer: `Thread(...).start()` and `Fiber(...).start()`
// both yield one, so a generic helper taking `Awaitable` waits on either.
// The trait is deliberately NOT the place for must-start semantics —
// start-optional lifecycles are legitimate for user primitives; must-start
// is a per-struct `#destroy` contract (see Thread.nm / Fiber.nm and
// ASYNC.md).
//
// `wait` takes the receiver by VALUE (bare `self`, not `ref self`): waiting
// never mutates the handle — it parks on the future the handle NAMES. That
// makes the bare `Awaitable` parameter (the T3 stack-copy box — zero
// malloc, caller keeps ownership) the sanctioned dispatch form; a `ref
// self` wait would be a mutating call, and through a boxed mv struct
// the mutation spellings cannot cross a borrow (STRUCT_PLAN_3 B4/T2).

/**
 * Something whose completion can be waited on (may park the current context)
 **/
pub trait Awaitable {
	func wait = (self) {
	}
}
